ose dangerous 3 in a row possibilities.\n\nI’ve considered blocking one of the extensions of those lines, for example at G9, K9, H6, or J6. If Black played G9, that gives them three in a row (I7-H8-G9). If Black played K9, they have three in a row (I7-J8-K9). To block this, I could play G9 to block the left-side threat or K9 to block the right side. Playing J6 or H6 would also stop Black from getting a free run.\n\nI have to choose between G9 or K9, and J6 and H6. If I play J6, Black still forms I7-J8-K9; this is not ideal. If I play H6, Black will form G9. H6 looks bad, since it will form a diagonal threat. I need a move that stops two threats at once. My immediate thought is G9, the best of the options I identified.\n\nPlaying G9:\n\n*   It physically blocks Black’s I7-H8-G9 diagonal.\n*   It connects to my H10 stone, creating a diagonal 2.\n*   Leaves K9 open, but the K9 is isolated, which is easier to control than several threats.\n\nTherefore, G9 is my choice. My move is to place a White (O) stone at column G, row 9.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows Black (X) holding a strong offensive position with a \'V\' shape anchored at I7, connecting to H8 and J8. This setup creates two immediate threats for Black to form "Open 3s" (three consecutive stones with open ends) along the diagonals: one at G9 (extending I7-H8) and another at K9 (extending I7-J8). White (O) is currently compressed in the center with stones at I8, I9, and H10. White lacks immediate winning combinations (VCF) and must prioritize defense to prevent Black from gaining a winning momentum.\n\nReason: White needs to block one of Black\'s major diagonal threats while ideally improving its own position.\n1.  **Threat Analysis**: Black threatens to play G9 or K9. Playing either would result in an Open 3, forcing White to block passively and giving Black the initiative to dictate the game.\n2.  **Candidate Moves**:\n    *   **G9**: This move blocks the I7-H8 diagonal. Crucially, it also connects with the White stone at H10, forming a diagonal pair (G9-H10). This connectivity strengthens White\'s shape and creates potential for future counter-attacks.\n    *   **K9**: This move blocks the I7-J8 diagonal. However, a stone at K9 would be isolated and does not connect well with existing White stones.\n    *   **I10**: Extends White\'s vertical line to a "Blocked 3" (I8-I9-I10), but this is too slow compared to Black\'s threats.\n3.  **Conclusion**: G9 is the superior move because it accomplishes two goals: it neutralizes a dangerous Black line and builds White\'s structural integrity by connecting with H10. Leaving G9 to Black would be more dangerous than leaving K9, as the G9 area is more clustered with other stones (H9, H8), offering Black more tactical possibilities.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 9}'}
