k of the black open three. So, H7 won\'t work.\n\nAfter considering everything, blocking at K9 is the best move. K9 is better, because it\'s closer to my existing stones.\n\nHere\'s the result I will submit: **K9**\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state presents a critical threat from Black. Black has a diagonal "Open 3" formed by stones at H6, I7, and J8. If Black is allowed to play at either end of this line (G5 or K9), they will form an "Open 4" (four stones in a row with both ends open), which guarantees a win on the subsequent turn. White has no immediate winning moves (4-in-a-row or 5-in-a-row) to counter this, nor any forcing threats that would compel Black to defend. Therefore, White\'s only option is to block this diagonal line immediately.\n\nReason: White must block the diagonal line H6-I7-J8. The two blocking candidates are G5 and K9. Blocking at K9 is superior for several reasons:\n1.  **Threat Prevention:** It successfully prevents Black from extending the Open 3 into an unstoppable Open 4.\n2.  **Positional Advantage:** K9 is located in an area with other White stones (I8, I9, H10), whereas G5 is more isolated. Placing a stone at K9 increases White\'s potential to form connecting lines in the future (e.g., with I9).\n3.  **Space Denial:** Blocking at K9 denies Black access to the larger open space on the right side of the board.\nWhile Black may still play H7 to form a vertical "Closed 4" (forcing White to block at H5), blocking the diagonal at K9 is the mandatory priority to avoid an immediate loss.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 9}'}
